[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Database Indexing: B-Trees vs. LSM-Trees"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Choosing the right storage engine architecture is one of the most critical decisions in system design. At the heart of most databases lie two primary data structures for indexing and storage: B-Trees and Log-Structured Merge-Trees (LSM-Trees). Understanding their internal mechanics, read/write performance profiles, and trade-offs is essential for designing scalable systems."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The B-Tree Architecture"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"B-Trees are the traditional gold standard for relational databases like PostgreSQL, MySQL (InnoDB), and SQLite. A B-Tree is a self-balancing tree data structure that maintains sorted data and allows searches, sequential access, insertions, and deletions in logarithmic time. Crucially, B-Trees modify data in place on disk."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Page-Oriented: B-Trees break down the database into fixed-size blocks or pages (typically 4KB to 16KB), mirroring the disk block size."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"In-Place Updates: When a record is updated or inserted, the database modifies the corresponding page directly on disk."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"High Read Performance: Reads require traversing a shallow tree, resulting in a predictable and low number of disk seeks (O(log N))."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"The LSM-Tree Architecture"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"LSM-Trees are optimized for write-heavy workloads and are commonly found in NoSQL databases such as Apache Cassandra, RocksDB, ScyllaDB, and LevelDB. Instead of modifying data in place, LSM-Trees append all writes sequentially to an immutable log and buffer updates in memory."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"MemTable: Writes first go to an in-memory sorted structure, such as a SkipList or red-black tree, providing extremely fast ingestion."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Write-Ahead Log (WAL): Writes are simultaneously appended to a disk-based WAL to ensure crash recovery and durability."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"SSTables (Sorted String Tables): When the MemTable reaches a threshold, it is flushed to disk as an immutable, sorted file known as an SSTable."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Compacting: Background processes periodically merge and compact SSTables to remove deleted or overwritten records and reclaim disk space."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Comparative Analysis: Reads vs. Writes"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"The fundamental architectural divergence leads to starkly different performance characteristics. B-Trees prioritize read efficiency, whereas LSM-Trees prioritize write throughput."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Write Amplification: B-Trees suffer from high write amplification because changing a single small value requires rewriting an entire disk page. LSM-Trees write sequentially, but experience write amplification during background compaction phases."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Read Amplification: B-Trees excel at reads because the path from root to leaf is short. LSM-Trees may need to check the MemTable and multiple SSTables on disk to find a single key, necessitating optimizations like Bloom filters."},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"System Design Implementation Example"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Below is a conceptual pseudo-code representation highlighting how an LSM-Tree manages writes via a MemTable and periodic flushing, contrasting with traditional random disk access."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"class LSMStorageEngine {
  constructor() {
    this.memTable = new SkipList();
    this.wal = new WriteAheadLog('storage.wal');
    this.sstables = [];
  }

  put(key, value) {
    this.wal.append({ key, value, type: 'PUT' });
    this.memTable.insert(key, value);

    if (this.memTable.size() >= THRESHOLD) {
      this.flushMemTableToSSTable();
    }
  }

  get(key) {
    if (this.memTable.contains(key)) {
      return this.memTable.get(key);
    }
    // Search through SSTables from newest to oldest
    for (let sstable of this.sstables) {
      if (sstable.mightContain(key) && sstable.find(key)) {
        return sstable.get(key);
      }
    }
    return null;
  }
}"},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary and Architectural Guidelines"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"When architecting a system, use B-Trees when your application demands heavy read performance, complex range queries, and strict consistency models with predictable latency profiles (e.g., financial ledgers, traditional CMS). Choose LSM-Trees when your application requires massive write throughput, high-frequency event ingestion, or time-series data storage (e.g., logging systems, IoT telemetry pipelines)."}]
